 "empty_stub_class": {
  "count": 62,
  "of_total": 92,
  "finding": "62 of 92 .rm files in the reference corpus are ZERO bytes -- the empty stubs written for unannotated pages of a PDF-backed document (legacy src/remarkable_spec/device/sync.py writes these). They are a NORMAL state, not corruption.",
  "legacy_behaviour": "parse_rm_file raises a bare EOFError with an empty message. Two thirds of a real corpus therefore crashes any naive page loop, and the exception says nothing about why.",
  "required_new_behaviour": "An empty stub must be a typed, expected outcome distinguishable from a truncated or malformed file -- a page with no ink, not a parse failure. A test must assert the distinction, because the OCR and render loops both iterate every page of a PDF-backed document and will meet this on most of them.",
  "sample_header_of_a_valid_file": "reMarkable .lines file, version=6       "
 },
 "screen_detection_note": "detect_screen classified 23 of the 30 renderable pages as Paper Pro (1620x2160) and 7 as reMarkable 2 (1404x1872), from one device's backup. Either the corpus genuinely spans two device generations or the stroke-extent heuristic is misclassifying sparse pages. The relocated code must reproduce whichever answer the legacy code gave -- these hashes encode it -- but the heuristic itself is worth a separate look."
}